## parameter-scaling rule (derived from the observed benign stream)
  B = p99.9 of single benign transfer size; T = max(B, 3 x p99 hourly volume);
  r = T / W  (a full-T window sustainable at steady state).
  rule-derived ledger: B=1,111 ETH ($3.45M), r=1.64 ETH/s, T=5,889 ETH ($18.32M)
  -> bound $18.62M (3.4% of Ronin), denied-or-delayed 48.66% of benign value, halts 0
#
## value-coverage table (the operator's real knob on a whale-dominated stream)
  smallest B admitting >= c of benign VALUE burst-free (count-quantiles of size
  barely move denied VALUE, because value concentrates in a few whales):
    coverage  50.0%  ->  B >= $2.74M (880 ETH)  ->  bound floor T + r·Δ >= $2.92M  (0.5% of Ronin)
    coverage  60.0%  ->  B >= $9.20M (2,957 ETH)  ->  bound floor T + r·Δ >= $9.38M  (1.7% of Ronin)
    coverage  75.0%  ->  B >= $155.81M (50,098 ETH)  ->  bound floor T + r·Δ >= $155.99M  (28.9% of Ronin)
    coverage  90.0%  ->  B >= $339.85M (109,278 ETH)  ->  bound floor T + r·Δ >= $340.04M  (63.0% of Ronin)
    coverage  99.0%  ->  B >= $396.11M (127,366 ETH)  ->  bound floor T + r·Δ >= $396.30M  (73.4% of Ronin)
#
## honest summary
  LOCKED ledger: bound $15.74M (2.9% of Ronin) is purchased at 50.48% of benign VALUE denied-or-delayed
  — benign whale transfers far above B dominate the utility cost (largest benign
  transfer $396.11M vs B = $3.11M). The Pareto frontier + scaling rule
  quantify exactly what admitting them costs in bound.
